<p>A storm that damages a home is stressful. A storm that damages a business adds payroll, tenants, customers, lenders, and lost revenue to the list. Commercial property insurance claims in San Marcos follow the same basic steps as residential claims, but the policies are more complex, more people have a stake in the outcome, and small details in the forms can make a large difference. This guide is for business owners, landlords, student-housing and multifamily owners, and homeowners associations in San Marcos and across Hays County.</p>

<p>We work as a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> representing policyholders on residential and <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>. Coverage always depends on your policy and the facts of the loss, and nothing here is legal advice.</p>

<h2>Who this applies to in San Marcos</h2>

<p>San Marcos is the Hays County seat on Interstate 35 (I-35), home to Texas State University, and part of a fast-growing corridor between Austin and San Antonio. That mix produces a wide range of commercial property:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Retail and service businesses</strong> along I-35 and in shopping centers, many under flat or low-slope roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Landlords and property managers</strong> with single-family rentals, duplexes, and small strip centers.</li>
<li><strong>Student-housing and multifamily owners</strong> near Texas State, where one storm can affect many units and many tenants.</li>
<li><strong>Homeowners associations and condominium associations</strong> responsible for common elements, clubhouses, fences, and in some cases roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Churches, offices, warehouses, and agricultural buildings</strong>, including metal buildings in the Hill Country west of town.</li>
</ul>

<p>Storm history shows why these owners should understand their coverage. The NOAA Storm Events Database records 4.25-inch hail on April 20, 2006 along I-35 near the San Marcos outlet malls, and 70 to 80 mph winds on January 13, 2007 that tore metal roofs off several buildings along I-35 in San Marcos (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=12001" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA NCEI event record</a>). On May 9, 2024, 70 mph gusts damaged the roof of a church in San Marcos (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1181414" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA NCEI event record</a>).</p>

<h2>Building vs. business personal property vs. business income</h2>

<p>Most commercial property policies are built from several coverage parts. Knowing which one applies to each piece of damage is the first step in a well-organized claim.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Coverage</th><th>What it generally covers</th><th>Common issues on claims</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Building</td><td>The structure, permanently installed fixtures, machinery, and equipment; often outdoor fixtures as defined</td><td>Roof scope, code items, matching, ACV vs. replacement cost, coinsurance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Business personal property (BPP)</td><td>Furniture, inventory, equipment, and certain tenant improvements and betterments owned by the business</td><td>Inventory records, valuation, separating owner and tenant property</td></tr>
<tr><td>Business income</td><td>Lost net income and continuing normal operating expenses during the period of restoration after covered physical damage</td><td>Waiting periods, proving lost income, defining when restoration should end</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extra expense</td><td>Reasonable costs to avoid or reduce a shutdown, such as temporary space or expedited repairs</td><td>Documenting why each cost was necessary</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rental income / loss of rents</td><td>Rent lost when units are untenantable after covered damage</td><td>Lease records, vacancy before the loss, unit-by-unit timelines</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ordinance or law</td><td>Added costs to comply with building codes when repairing covered damage, up to a limit</td><td>Low or absent limits; showing what the code requires</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Business income claims deserve special attention. They usually require profit and loss statements, tax returns, sales records, and payroll data, along with a clear explanation of how the damage affected operations. Many forms include a waiting period before coverage applies. Start gathering financial records early, and keep a simple log of closures, reduced hours, and extra costs.</p>

<h2>Coinsurance: how underinsurance can shrink a payment</h2>

<p>Many commercial property policies include a <strong>coinsurance clause</strong>. In plain terms, it requires you to carry insurance equal to at least a set percentage, often 80%, 90%, or 100%, of the property’s value at the time of loss. If you carry less, the insurer may reduce the payment on a partial loss in proportion to the shortfall.</p>

<div class="callout callout-gold"><span class="callout-title">Hypothetical example (for illustration only)</span>
<p>Suppose a building has a replacement cost value of $1,000,000 at the time of loss, and the policy has an 80% coinsurance clause. The required amount of insurance is $800,000. The owner carried only $600,000.</p>
<p>A hailstorm causes $100,000 of covered damage. Under a typical coinsurance formula, the insurer divides the amount carried by the amount required ($600,000 ÷ $800,000 = 75%) and applies that to the loss: $100,000 × 75% = $75,000, less the deductible. If the deductible were $5,000, the payment would be $70,000 instead of $95,000.</p>
<p>These numbers are invented to show the math. Your policy’s wording, valuation basis, and deductible terms control how coinsurance applies.</p></div>

<p>Values in the I-35 corridor have changed quickly, so limits set years ago may not keep up with current construction costs. Some policies offer an agreed value option that suspends coinsurance for a period, so it is worth reviewing your declarations with your agent before a loss. After a loss, a careful review of the insurer’s valuation can matter, because the coinsurance calculation depends on it.</p>

