<h2>Low-slope and metal roofs on commercial buildings</h2>

<p>Roofs drive most commercial storm claims in Central Texas, and commercial roofs differ from residential shingle roofs in ways that affect a claim.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/hail-dented-metal-roof.webp" alt="Standing-seam metal roof with hail dents across the panels" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Hail dents on metal roofing often raise questions about cosmetic damage exclusions.</figcaption></figure>

<h3>Low-slope membrane roofs</h3>
<p>Flat and low-slope roofs are common on retail, office, and multifamily buildings along I-35. Membranes such as TPO, modified bitumen, and built-up roofs can be damaged by hail in ways that are hard to see, such as fractures in the membrane, crushed insulation underneath, or damage at seams and flashings. Testing and core samples are sometimes used to evaluate the roof system. Rooftop HVAC units, curbs, skylights, and drains should be inspected as part of the same claim.</p>

<h3>Metal roofs</h3>
<p>Standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs are common on warehouses, churches, barns, and Hill Country buildings. Hail can dent panels, and wind can lift or peel them, as several NOAA reports in Hays County describe. Many commercial policies include endorsements that exclude cosmetic damage to metal roofing, meaning dents that do not affect function. Whether a particular dent is cosmetic or functional, such as one that affects seams, fasteners, coatings, or water-shedding, is often the core question on these claims.</p>

<h3>Codes and permits</h3>
<p>The City of San Marcos applies the 2021 International Codes, including the IBC, IECC, and IEBC, in effect since April 1, 2023. Reroofing a commercial building can bring up code items such as insulation or roof drainage requirements. Whether the insurer pays for those upgrades depends on ordinance or law coverage. In unincorporated Hays County, the county applies the 2018 International Fire Code to new commercial and multifamily buildings and major remodels. See our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page for details and links.</p>

<h2>Documentation for a commercial claim</h2>

<p>Commercial insurers typically ask for more documentation than on a homeowners claim. Being ready can help keep the claim moving. Useful items include:</p>
<ul>
<li>The complete policy, including declarations, all coverage forms, and endorsements.</li>
<li>Dated photos and video of all damage, inside and out, before cleanup where safe.</li>
<li>Building plans, roof reports, and maintenance and repair records.</li>
<li>Inventory and equipment lists with purchase records.</li>
<li>Financial records for business income: profit and loss statements, tax returns, and sales and payroll reports.</li>
<li>Leases, rent rolls, and tenant notices for rental and multifamily properties.</li>
<li>Invoices for emergency repairs and mitigation, such as tarping, water extraction, and board-up.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> is a good starting point, and our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> tool can help you find weather records for your date of loss.</p>

<h2>Tenant, lender, and association issues</h2>

<p>Commercial claims often involve more than one insured party, and the paperwork that connects them matters.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Leases.</strong> Commercial leases typically say who must insure the building, who insures improvements, who repairs what, and whether rent abates after damage. Read the insurance and casualty sections early.</li>
<li><strong>Tenant improvements.</strong> Improvements a tenant paid for may be covered under the tenant’s BPP coverage, not the landlord’s building coverage. Coordinate so items are not missed or claimed twice.</li>
<li><strong>Student and multifamily tenants.</strong> Tenants’ belongings are usually covered by their own renters policies, if they have one. The owner’s policy may cover loss of rents while units are being repaired.</li>
<li><strong>HOAs and condos.</strong> The association’s master policy and governing documents define which parts are common elements. Unit owners often have separate policies for their interiors and personal property.</li>
<li><strong>Mortgagees.</strong> Lenders are often named on claim checks and may control the release of funds. Ask for their requirements in writing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Deadlines that apply to commercial claims</h2>

<p>Deadlines do not pause because a claim is complicated. Keep these in view:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Notice.</strong> Most policies require prompt notice of a loss. Report it as soon as you reasonably can.</li>
<li><strong>Proof of loss.</strong> Some policies require a sworn proof of loss within a set time after the insurer asks for it.</li>
<li><strong>Prompt Payment rules.</strong> Texas Insurance Code <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chapter 542, Subchapter B</a> sets timelines for insurers to acknowledge, decide, and pay. Eligible surplus lines insurers, common in commercial property, have different timeframes. See <a href="/why-property-insurance-claims-are-delayed-texas/">why property insurance claims are delayed in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Replacement cost.</strong> Many policies pay actual cash value first and the rest after repairs are completed within a set period.</li>
<li><strong>Suit limitations.</strong> Many Texas property policies limit the time to file suit, often two years and one day from when the claim accrued. Talk to a Texas attorney about any legal deadline.</li>
</ul>

<p>Flood is a separate matter. Commercial property policies generally exclude flood, and flood coverage comes from the NFIP or private flood insurers (<a href="https://www.floodsmart.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FloodSmart</a>). New FEMA flood maps for the Blanco River, the San Marcos River, and several creeks took effect in San Marcos on January 17, 2025, so it is worth checking your building’s current zone.</p>

<h2>Getting help with a San Marcos commercial claim</h2>

<p>A business owner’s time is usually best spent running the business, not managing an insurance file. That is the main reason commercial clients contact a public adjuster in San Marcos. A public adjuster can document the loss, prepare a detailed estimate, organize business income records, and handle communication with the insurer on behalf of the named insured. If you are weighing outside help for a commercial property claim, a San Marcos public adjuster can review your policy and the insurer’s position at no cost. Request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]]. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
